When a partitioned input has a much smaller output, investigate a region summary as a live task class instead of assuming a same-size edit or stamp. Identify separator rows/columns, compute the number and arrangement of regions, and associate each region with the corresponding output cell. Inspect marker presence, marker color/identity, count, and position within each region as candidate encodings. Verify the exact output—including default values for empty regions—against every demonstration before transfer. Output dimensions alone do not establish the mapping. Before spending another demo request, list at least two candidate encodings and state what observation in the next pair would distinguish them.

In a demo with sparse markers embedded in a regular partitioned canvas, the compact output preserved a 6×3 region arrangement and represented localized marker colors at corresponding output positions; most empty positions retained the canvas color. Treat this as a diagnostic pattern, not a universal rule: confirm separators and correspondence in the current task.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/held-out/play/users/u040/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-delta-discipline/references/compact-region-mapping.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-ledger", "file": "references/periodic-field-inference.md", "content": "# Periodic-field hypothesis from heterogeneous demos

When a demo appears unrelated to another by palette, dimensions, or local geometry, first inspect whether each input nevertheless encodes an ordered sequence or lattice that is expanded into a full periodic output. For a periodic candidate, independently derive the period, symbol/color order, direction, and phase/origin from the input markers and output. Verify the complete generated grid against every demo, including row/column transitions; a visually plausible periodic fill is not evidence by itself. Heterogeneity alone is not grounds either to dismiss a shared abstract rule or to repeatedly request demonstrations without a discriminating question.

This is a diagnostic technique, not a solved task-specific mapping. In the reviewed stream, the demos included sparse color tracks and an already periodic output; the test likewise contained diagonal color tracks, but no transfer rule was rigorously established before the next demo request. Future analyses should extract and validate the proposed sequence-to-field mapping before submission.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/held-out/play/users/u040/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-ledger/references/periodic-field-inference.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-task-delta-inference", "file": "references/compact-output-reassessment-after-errors.md", "content": "# Compact-output reassessment after incorrect submissions

When inputs are partitioned by repeated separator rows or columns but demos produce much smaller outputs, dimension ratios help identify possible structure but do not establish the encoding. Keep distinct hypotheses for (a) summarizing interior regions, (b) recording separator/boundary markers at their intersections, and (c) selecting only rows or columns with special markers. For each demo, write down the exact partition counts and map candidate input coordinates or blocks to each output cell, including ordering. Reproduce every output exactly before transferring the map.

After an incorrect compact-output submission, do not merely alter colors or copy another visually salient set of markers. Reopen the task class and output representation, and re-derive dimensions and correspondence from all demos. Repeated, heterogeneous demos may share only an abstract structure; spend another demo only when two explicit mappings predict a materially different discriminating observation.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/held-out/play/users/u040/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-task-delta-inference/references/compact-output-reassessment-after-errors.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-ledger", "file": "references/periodic-field-cross-demo-test.md", "content": "# Periodic-field hypothesis test across heterogeneous demos

When a demo output is a dense repeating field but other examples look unlike it, neither force a shared rule from visual similarity nor dismiss the periodic case as unrelated. Treat the pattern as a testable abstraction: record the output's row/column period, color cycle ordering, direction, and phase/origin; compare these with the input's sparse diagonal tracks, marker sequence, or stripe ordering. Distinguish period from phase (a correct cycle with wrong origin still fails exact replay).

Replay the proposed generator against every complete demo. Include cases where the output is uniform or striped: these may be degenerate instances of a shared periodic generator, or evidence against it, and must not be ignored. A test input that visually resembles a periodic encoding only motivates the hypothesis; it does not establish mapping. If no deterministic cycle/phase rule reproduces all outputs, label it unresolved rather than submitting cosmetic rotations/reversals or repeatedly requesting demos without a discriminating prediction.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/held-out/play/users/u040/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-ledger/references/periodic-field-cross-demo-test.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-task-delta-inference", "file": "references/task-29b1b5eb-edge-extension.md", "content": "# Edge-extension evidence from a heterogeneous ARC stream

In a stream instance with varied palettes and grid sizes, demonstrations consistently modified only cells immediately to the right of an existing horizontal same-color run, recoloring the formerly background suffix to color 1. Examples included short left-edge runs extended to the right boundary and right-edge-adjacent bars in different locations; complete bars remained unchanged. A test with sparse/colorful motifs still followed this family: only the end of the horizontal run at the right edge was converted, leaving the rest intact.

Process lesson: do not spend the full demo budget automatically. Extract each pair's exact changed coordinates, compare with all unchanged candidate runs, and formulate a measurable selector (e.g., a bar ending short of the right boundary versus full-width bars) before another request. Even when a rule is evident, validate the predicted row and exact suffix length against every demo. Keep the session-specific rule as evidence, not as a universal task-ID mapping.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/held-out/play/users/u040/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-task-delta-inference/references/task-29b1b5eb-edge-extension.md"}
